{"entity": "Offline Password Cracking", "type": "AttackTechnique", "description": "Offline Password Cracking refers to a cybersecurity threat wherein attackers recover plaintext passwords from compromised hash values stored locally, without direct network interaction. This technique employs brute-force or dictionary attacks against pre-intercepted credentials that have been stolen via external breaches, exfiltrated data, or post-exploitation activities. Attackers utilize local computational resources to systematically compare guessed passwords against hash results, often leveraging precomputed wordlists, custom rules, or exhaustive password combinations to uncover valid credentials. Key strategies include comparing extensive dictionaries with captured hashes or iterating through all possible password combinations to find matches. While it serves as a valid offensive security testing methodology to evaluate the strength of hashing and password policies, it is frequently employed by adversaries to gain unauthorized system access by validating cracked credentials against harvested datasets. This poses a significant security risk to systems utilizing weak, predictable, or reused passwords, underscoring the necessity of robust password policies, computationally intensive hashing algorithms like bcrypt, and mitigation controls such as account lockouts or rate limiting to defend against such attacks.<SEP>A technique used to recover password hashes through computational methods without live network access.<SEP>A technique that uses local computational resources to defeat weak authentication controls without direct network contact.<SEP>An action that uses computational tools to attempt to discover plaintext passwords from hashed credentials conducted outside the target system.<SEP>Technique using dictionary or brute-force attacks on password hashes obtained via credential exposure or hashed credentials leaked through verbose errors.<SEP>Brute-force or dictionary attack on captured password hashes to retrieve plaintext credentials.<SEP>A technique involving password hashes or logs, focusing on cracking weak or reused passwords."}
{"entity": "Token Signing Ability", "type": "AttackerCapability", "description": "The capability to generate valid signed tokens or forge existing ones without detection.<SEP>Capability to create, forge, or modify tokens (such as JWTs) without access to the secret key or proper validation.<SEP>The capability to forge or modify valid authentication tokens that the server will accept, often leading to unauthorized access.<SEP>An existing attacker capability to generate, sign, or forge valid credentials or tokens required for specific APIs or services.<SEP>An ability to create valid tokens mimicking legitimate signing keys to bypass session or authentication checks.<SEP>The attacker's capability to generate, forge, or manipulate valid JWT tokens due to knowledge of the secret or algorithm"}
{"entity": "Accessible Upload Handler", "type": "PreconditionEnvironment", "description": "When the web application provides a directory or endpoint where files can be uploaded for resources such as user avatars or themes.<SEP>Accessible Upload Handler is a web endpoint or component configured to accept and process user-uploaded files or data through form interfaces or direct API calls, saving content to the server's file system for later web access. While fulfilling an essential user-facing function for content submission such as images and documents, these handlers become significant security risks when inadequately secured. Exposed configurations lacking authentication, permission checks, or input validation allow attackers — whether unauthenticated or authenticated — to interact with the handler. This vulnerability serves as a critical attack vector, frequently enabling malicious actors to upload and install webshells, execute remote code, exfiltrate data, or fully compromise the server. Consequently, an Accessible Upload Handler describes a target condition where a reachable upload endpoint accepts user-supplied data without proper validation or extension controls, permitting the unauthorized storage of malicious artifacts on the system.<SEP>A web endpoint or interface that allows receiving, storing, or processing uploaded files from unauthenticated or low-privilege users.<SEP>A web endpoint or functionality that accepts and processes user-uploaded files.<SEP>A target file-upload component reachable via API, web form or service, subject to insufficient validation.<SEP>A target component or endpoint that accepts and processes user-uploaded files without adequate validation.<SEP>An upload endpoint accessible to an attacker that lacks sufficient restriction on file type or size."}
