{"reference_id": "1", "content": "# Methodology Scenario\n\n## Methodology Scope\n\nThis document contains reusable web application testing methodology.\n\n## WSTG Scenario Anchor\n\n- WSTG ID: WSTG-ATHZ-02\n- WSTG title: Testing for Bypassing Authorization Schema\n- WSTG category: Authorization Testing (ATHZ)\n- Source file: data/lightrag/inputs/wstg_raw/4-Web_Application_Security_Testing/05-Authorization_Testing/02-Testing_for_Bypassing_Authorization_Schema.md\n- Canonical aliases: WSTG-ATHZ-02, Testing for Bypassing Authorization Schema, Bypassing Authorization Schema, Authorization Testing, ATHZ\n- Multi-hop retrieval target: features, technologies, vulnerability families, input vectors, payload patterns, observable signals, preconditions, and methodology steps in this document map back to WSTG-ATHZ-02.\n\n## Ontology Query Anchors\n\nPurpose: Phase 2 ontology anchors.\n\n- PreconditionEnvironment: Tenant Scoped Object IDs\n- VulnerabilityClass: Authorization Schema Bypass\n- AttackTechnique: Forced Browsing To Unauthorized Function\n- ObservableSignal: Authorization Response Difference\n- Retrieval link: matching profile evidence should retrieve WSTG scenario WSTG-ATHZ-02.\n\n## Overview\n\nPurpose: Scenario summary, vulnerability framing, impact, and core testing purpose.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-02; WSTG title Testing for Bypassing Authorization Schema; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nThis test case focuses on identifying authorization weaknesses where an authenticated\nuser is able to access resources or perform actions beyond their assigned permissions,\nincluding horizontal and vertical privilege escalation.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-02; WSTG title Testing for Bypassing Authorization Schema; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nWhile some checks may include scenarios such as direct access to protected resources\nwithout an active session or after logout, the primary intent of this test is to\nvalidate that authorization controls are correctly enforced for authenticated users\nand roles.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-02; WSTG title Testing for Bypassing Authorization Schema; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nDetailed unauthenticated and post-authentication scenarios are covered in the\nHow to Test section.\n\n## Test Objectives\n\nPurpose: Explicit objectives a tester should satisfy for this WSTG scenario.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-02; WSTG title Testing for Bypassing Authorization Schema; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\n- Assess if unauthenticated, horizontal, or vertical access is possible.\n\n## Attack Methods\n\nPurpose: Concrete testing procedures, probes, exploitation approaches, and technique variants.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-02; WSTG title Testing for Bypassing Authorization Schema; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nFor every function, specific role, or request that the application executes, it is necessary to verify:\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-02; WSTG title Testing for Bypassing Authorization Schema; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\n- Is it possible to access resources that should be accessible to a user that holds a different identity with the same role or privilege?\n- Is it possible to operate functions on resources that should be accessible to a user that holds a different identity?\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-02; WSTG title Testing for Bypassing Authorization Schema; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nFor each role:\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-02; WSTG title Testing for Bypassing Authorization Schema; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\n1. Register or generate two users with identical privileges.\n2. Establish and keep two different sessions active (one for each user).\n3. For every request, change the relevant request input fields and the session identifier from token one to token two and diagnose the responses for each token.\n4. An application will be considered vulnerable if the responses are the same, contain same private data or indicate successful operation on other users' resource or data.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-02; WSTG title Testing for Bypassing Authorization Schema; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nValid and legitimate response:\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-02; WSTG title Testing for Bypassing Authorization Schema; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nFurther questions or considerations would go in the following direction:\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-02; WSTG title Testing for Bypassing Authorization Schema; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\n- What happens if a non-administrative user tries to execute that request?\n- Will the user be created?\n- If so, can the new user use their privileges?\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-02; WSTG title Testing for Bypassing Authorization Schema; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nVarious applications setup resource controls based on user roles. Let's take an example resumes or CVs (curriculum vitae) uploaded on a careers form to an S3 bucket.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-02; WSTG title Testing for Bypassing Authorization Schema; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nAs a normal user, try accessing the location of those files. If you are able to retrieve them, modify them, or delete them, then the application is vulnerable.\n\n## Prerequisites And Environment\n\nPurpose: Target conditions, reachable inputs, side channels, and applicability constraints."}
{"reference_id": "1", "content": "## Canonical Relation Anchors\n\nPurpose: provide stable graph targets so multi-hop retrieval can resolve abstract web features to canonical WSTG test cases.\n\n- WSTG scenario WSTG-ATHZ-02 has title Testing for Bypassing Authorization Schema.\n- WSTG scenario WSTG-ATHZ-02 belongs to category Authorization Testing (ATHZ).\n- WSTG category Authorization Testing contains WSTG scenario WSTG-ATHZ-02.\n- Methodology for Testing for Bypassing Authorization Schema maps to WSTG scenario WSTG-ATHZ-02.\n- WSTG scenario WSTG-ATHZ-02 contains test objectives, attack methods, prerequisites, defenses, detections, payload examples, and observable signals.\n- Concept Testing for Bypassing Authorization Schema maps to WSTG scenario WSTG-ATHZ-02.\n- Concept Bypassing Authorization Schema maps to WSTG scenario WSTG-ATHZ-02.\n- PreconditionEnvironment anchors Tenant Scoped Object IDs map to WSTG scenario WSTG-ATHZ-02.\n- VulnerabilityClass anchors Authorization Schema Bypass map to WSTG scenario WSTG-ATHZ-02.\n- AttackTechnique anchors Forced Browsing To Unauthorized Function map to WSTG scenario WSTG-ATHZ-02.\n- ObservableSignal anchors Authorization Response Difference map to WSTG scenario WSTG-ATHZ-02.\n\n## Relation Briefs\n\nPurpose: preserve source-grounded operational claims connecting testing methods, target conditions, defensive behavior, weakness categories, and payload examples.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-02; WSTG title Testing for Bypassing Authorization Schema; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nThis test case focuses on identifying authorization weaknesses where an authenticated\nuser is able to access resources or perform actions beyond their assigned permissions,\nincluding horizontal and vertical privilege escalation.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-02; WSTG title Testing for Bypassing Authorization Schema; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nWhile some checks may include scenarios such as direct access to protected resources\nwithout an active session or after logout, the primary intent of this test is to\nvalidate that authorization controls are correctly enforced for authenticated users\nand roles.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-02; WSTG title Testing for Bypassing Authorization Schema; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nDetailed unauthenticated and post-authentication scenarios are covered in the\nHow to Test section.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-02; WSTG title Testing for Bypassing Authorization Schema; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\n- Assess if unauthenticated, horizontal, or vertical access is possible.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-02; WSTG title Testing for Bypassing Authorization Schema; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\n- Access resources and conduct operations without login. - Direct page request (forced browsing)\n- Access resources and conduct operations horizontally.\n- Access resources and conduct operations vertically.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-02; WSTG title Testing for Bypassing Authorization Schema; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nFor request capture tooling, using a adddon for Access Control Testing allows testers to determine which parts of the application are available to anonymous users, and identify potential access control issues.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-02; WSTG title Testing for Bypassing Authorization Schema; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nIf the attacker's response contain the data of the `example_user`, then the application is vulnerable for lateral movement attacks, where a user can read or write other user's data.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-02; WSTG title Testing for Bypassing Authorization Schema; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nEmploy the least privilege principles on the users, roles, and resources to ensure that no unauthorized access occurs."}
{"reference_id": "2", "content": "# Methodology Scenario\n\n## Methodology Scope\n\nThis document contains reusable web application testing methodology.\n\n## WSTG Scenario Anchor\n\n- WSTG ID: WSTG-ATHZ-03\n- WSTG title: Testing for Privilege Escalation\n- WSTG category: Authorization Testing (ATHZ)\n- Source file: data/lightrag/inputs/wstg_raw/4-Web_Application_Security_Testing/05-Authorization_Testing/03-Testing_for_Privilege_Escalation.md\n- Canonical aliases: WSTG-ATHZ-03, Testing for Privilege Escalation, Privilege Escalation, Authorization Testing, ATHZ\n- Multi-hop retrieval target: features, technologies, vulnerability families, input vectors, payload patterns, observable signals, preconditions, and methodology steps in this document map back to WSTG-ATHZ-03.\n\n## Overview\n\nPurpose: Scenario summary, vulnerability framing, impact, and core testing purpose.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-03; WSTG title Testing for Privilege Escalation; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nThis section describes the issue of escalating privileges from one stage to another. During this phase, the tester should verify that it is not possible for a user to modify their privileges or roles inside the application in ways that could allow privilege escalation attacks.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-03; WSTG title Testing for Privilege Escalation; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nPrivilege escalation occurs when a user gets access to more resources or functionality than they are normally allowed, and such elevation or changes should have been prevented by the application. This is usually caused by a flaw in the application. The result is that the application performs actions with more privileges than those intended by the developer or system administrator.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-03; WSTG title Testing for Privilege Escalation; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nThe degree of escalation depends on what privileges the attacker is authorized to possess, and what privileges can be obtained in a successful exploit. For example, a programming error that allows a user to gain extra privilege after successful authentication limits the degree of escalation, because the user is already authorized to hold some privilege. Likewise, a remote attacker gaining superuser privilege without any authentication presents a greater degree of escalation.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-03; WSTG title Testing for Privilege Escalation; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nUsually, people refer to *vertical escalation* when it is possible to access resources granted to more privileged accounts (e.g., acquiring administrative privileges for the application), and to *horizontal escalation* when it is possible to access resources granted to a similarly configured account (e.g., in an online banking application, accessing information related to a different user).\n\n## Test Objectives\n\nPurpose: Explicit objectives a tester should satisfy for this WSTG scenario.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-03; WSTG title Testing for Privilege Escalation; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\n- Identify injection points related to privilege manipulation.\n- Fuzz or otherwise attempt to bypass security measures.\n\n## Attack Methods\n\nPurpose: Concrete testing procedures, probes, exploitation approaches, and technique variants.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-03; WSTG title Testing for Privilege Escalation; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nIn every portion of the application where a user can create information in the database (e.g., making a payment, adding a contact, or sending a message), can receive information (statement of account, order details, etc.), or delete information (drop users, messages, etc.), it is necessary to record that functionality. The tester should try to access such functions as another user in order to verify if it is possible to access a function that should not be permitted by the user's role/privilege (but might be permitted as another user).\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-03; WSTG title Testing for Privilege Escalation; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nA vertical authorization bypass is specific to the case that an attacker obtains a role higher than their own. Testing for this bypass focuses on verifying how the vertical authorization schema has been implemented for each role. For every function, page, specific role, or request that the application executes, it is necessary to verify if it is possible to:\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-03; WSTG title Testing for Privilege Escalation; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\n- Access resources that should be accessible only to a higher role user.\n- Operate functions on resources that should be operative only by a user that holds a higher or specific role identity.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-03; WSTG title Testing for Privilege Escalation; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nFor each role:\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-03; WSTG title Testing for Privilege Escalation; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\n1. Register a user.\n2. Establish and maintain two different sessions based on the two different roles.\n3. For every request, change the session identifier from the original to another role's session identifier and evaluate the responses for each.\n4. An application will be considered vulnerable if the weaker privileged session contains the same data, or indicate successful operations on higher privileged functions.\n\n## Prerequisites And Environment\n\nPurpose: Target conditions, reachable inputs, side channels, and applicability constraints."}
{"reference_id": "3", "content": "# Methodology Scenario\n\n## Methodology Scope\n\nThis document contains reusable web application testing methodology.\n\n## WSTG Scenario Anchor\n\n- WSTG ID: WSTG-APIT-02\n- WSTG title: API Broken Object Level Authorization\n- WSTG category: API Testing (APIT)\n- Source file: data/lightrag/inputs/wstg_raw/4-Web_Application_Security_Testing/12-API_Testing/02-API_Broken_Object_Level_Authorization.md\n- Canonical aliases: WSTG-APIT-02, API Broken Object Level Authorization, API Testing, APIT\n- Multi-hop retrieval target: features, technologies, vulnerability families, input vectors, payload patterns, observable signals, preconditions, and methodology steps in this document map back to WSTG-APIT-02.\n\n## Ontology Query Anchors\n\nPurpose: Phase 2 ontology anchors.\n\n- TechnologyStack: REST API, GraphQL API\n- PreconditionEnvironment: Tenant Scoped Object IDs\n- VulnerabilityClass: Broken Object-Level Authorization\n- AttackTechnique: Object ID Tampering\n- ObservableSignal: Adjacent Account ID Accessible\n- Retrieval link: matching profile evidence should retrieve WSTG scenario WSTG-APIT-02.\n\n## Overview\n\nPurpose: Scenario summary, vulnerability framing, impact, and core testing purpose.\n\nAPI Broken Object Level Authorization tests whether a REST API or GraphQL API enforces authorization for every object accessed by the client. The core target condition is Tenant Scoped Object IDs: object identifiers appear in API requests and must be checked against the current account, tenant, role, and ownership rules.\n\n## Test Objectives\n\nPurpose: Explicit objectives a tester should satisfy for this WSTG scenario.\n\n- Determine whether users can access or modify objects they do not own by changing object identifiers.\n- Test object-level authorization across read, create, update, patch, delete, and bulk-list behavior.\n- Confirm whether Broken Object-Level Authorization is observable through cross-account or cross-tenant access.\n\n## Target Conditions And Object References\n\n- Tenant Scoped Object IDs may appear as URL path values, query values, JSON body fields, GraphQL ID arguments, GUIDs, numeric IDs, tokens, or nested object references.\n- Useful test setup uses at least two accounts in different ownership contexts, such as account A owning a resource and account B attempting access.\n- Candidate object identifiers can come from API documentation, captured traffic, user-visible data, list responses, predictable sequences, or generated test data.\n\n## Concrete Test Methods And Observable Signals\n\n- Perform Object ID Tampering by changing an object identifier in a captured request, then replaying the request under a different account or tenant.\n- Test common HTTP methods: `GET` for unauthorized reads, `POST` or `PUT` for unauthorized writes, `PATCH` for partial updates, and `DELETE` for unauthorized deletion.\n- For REST API paths, compare requests such as `GET /api/users/123/profile` and `GET /api/users/124/profile` under different accounts.\n- For GraphQL API requests, modify ID arguments such as `query { user(id: \"124\") { name email } }` and compare authorization behavior.\n- Test bulk-list routes such as `GET /api/users` to determine whether responses include only authorized objects.\n- Adjacent Account ID Accessible is an observable signal when a modified identifier returns another user's data, changes another user's object, or returns `200 OK` where `401 Unauthorized` or `403 Forbidden` is expected.\n- Inconsistent authorization responses across similar route locations indicate incomplete object-level enforcement.\n\n## Defenses And Detections\n\n- Enforce server-side object ownership checks for every request that reads or changes an object.\n- Bind each object access decision to the authenticated account, tenant, role, and action being performed.\n- Apply least privilege so each user can access only objects required for the user's role.\n- Prefer non-sequential identifiers such as UUIDs to slow enumeration, while still enforcing authorization on every object access.\n- Log cross-account object-access denials and high-volume object ID probing as authorization abuse signals.\n\n## Canonical Relation Anchors\n\nPurpose: provide stable graph targets so multi-hop retrieval can resolve abstract web features to canonical WSTG test cases.\n\n- WSTG scenario WSTG-APIT-02 has title API Broken Object Level Authorization.\n- WSTG scenario WSTG-APIT-02 belongs to category API Testing (APIT).\n- WSTG category API Testing contains WSTG scenario WSTG-APIT-02.\n- Methodology for API Broken Object Level Authorization maps to WSTG scenario WSTG-APIT-02.\n- WSTG scenario WSTG-APIT-02 contains test objectives, attack methods, prerequisites, defenses, detections, payload examples, and observable signals.\n- Concept API Broken Object Level Authorization maps to WSTG scenario WSTG-APIT-02.\n- TechnologyStack anchors REST API, GraphQL API map to WSTG scenario WSTG-APIT-02.\n- PreconditionEnvironment anchors Tenant Scoped Object IDs map to WSTG scenario WSTG-APIT-02.\n- VulnerabilityClass anchors Broken Object-Level Authorization map to WSTG scenario WSTG-APIT-02.\n- AttackTechnique anchors Object ID Tampering map to WSTG scenario WSTG-APIT-02.\n- ObservableSignal anchors Adjacent Account ID Accessible map to WSTG scenario WSTG-APIT-02.\n\n## Relation Briefs\n\nPurpose: preserve source-grounded operational claims connecting testing methods, target conditions, defensive behavior, weakness categories, and payload examples."}
{"reference_id": "1", "content": "Anchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-02; WSTG title Testing for Bypassing Authorization Schema; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nAs a normal user, try accessing the location of those files. If you are able to retrieve them, modify them, or delete them, then the application is vulnerable.\n\n## Prerequisites And Environment\n\nPurpose: Target conditions, reachable inputs, side channels, and applicability constraints.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-02; WSTG title Testing for Bypassing Authorization Schema; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nThis process can be automated if you have a list of all route locations with tools like ffuf, gobuster, request capture tooling, and request capture tooling Intruder.\n\n## Defenses And Detections\n\nPurpose: Controls, validation behavior, mitigations, detection signals, and defensive limits.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-02; WSTG title Testing for Bypassing Authorization Schema; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nWhen a web application does not properly enforce access control mechanisms, sensitive resources become exposed, allowing unauthenticated users to view them. For example, if a user directly requests a different page via forced browsing, that page may not check the authorization of the anonymous user before granting access. Attempt to directly access a protected page through the address bar in your browser to test using this method.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-02; WSTG title Testing for Bypassing Authorization Schema; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nFor request capture tooling, using a adddon for Access Control Testing allows testers to determine which parts of the application are available to anonymous users, and identify potential access control issues.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-02; WSTG title Testing for Bypassing Authorization Schema; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nThis behavior can be leveraged in a situation in which the application is behind a component that applies access control restriction based on the request URL.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-02; WSTG title Testing for Bypassing Authorization Schema; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nThe kind of access control restriction based on the request URL can be, for example, blocking access from internet to an administration console exposed on `/console` or `/admin`.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-02; WSTG title Testing for Bypassing Authorization Schema; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nTo detect the support for the header `X-Original-URL` or `X-Rewrite-URL`, the following steps can be applied.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-02; WSTG title Testing for Bypassing Authorization Schema; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nOnce the support for the header `X-Original-URL` or `X-Rewrite-URL` was validated then the tentative of bypass against the access control restriction can be leveraged by sending the expected request to the application but specifying a URL \"allowed\" by the frontend component as the main request URL and specifying the real target URL in the `X-Original-URL` or `X-Rewrite-URL` header depending on the one supported. If both are supported then try one after the other to verify for which header the bypass is effective.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-02; WSTG title Testing for Bypassing Authorization Schema; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nEmploy the least privilege principles on the users, roles, and resources to ensure that no unauthorized access occurs.\n\n## Code And Payload Examples\n\nPurpose: Payloads, URLs, SQL snippets, command examples, and concrete request material.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-02; WSTG title Testing for Bypassing Authorization Schema; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\n- Access resources and conduct operations without login. - Direct page request (forced browsing)\n- Access resources and conduct operations horizontally.\n- Access resources and conduct operations vertically.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-02; WSTG title Testing for Bypassing Authorization Schema; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nFor example, suppose that the `viewSettings` function is part of every account menu of the application with the same role, and it is possible to access it by requesting the following URL: `<scheme>://<host>/<path>`. Then, the following HTTP request is generated when calling the `viewSettings` function:\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-02; WSTG title Testing for Bypassing Authorization Schema; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\n```http\nPOST /account/viewSettings HTTP/1.1\nHost: www.example.com\n[other HTTP headers]\nCookie: SessionID=USER_SESSION\n\nusername=example_user\n```\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-02; WSTG title Testing for Bypassing Authorization Schema; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\n```html\nHTTP1.1 200 OK\n[other HTTP headers]\n\n{\n  \"username\": \"example_user\",\n  \"email\": \"example@email.com\",\n  \"address\": \"Example Address\"\n}\n```\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-02; WSTG title Testing for Bypassing Authorization Schema; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nThe attacker may try and execute that request with the same `username` parameter:"}
{"reference_id": "4", "content": "# Methodology Scenario\n\n## Methodology Scope\n\nThis document contains reusable web application testing methodology.\n\n## WSTG Scenario Anchor\n\n- WSTG ID: WSTG-APIT-04\n- WSTG title: API Broken Function Level Authorization\n- WSTG category: API Testing (APIT)\n- Source file: data/lightrag/inputs/wstg_raw/4-Web_Application_Security_Testing/12-API_Testing/04-API_Broken_Function_Level_Authorization.md\n- Canonical aliases: WSTG-APIT-04, API Broken Function Level Authorization, API Testing, APIT\n- Multi-hop retrieval target: features, technologies, vulnerability families, input vectors, payload patterns, observable signals, preconditions, and methodology steps in this document map back to WSTG-APIT-04.\n\n## Overview\n\nPurpose: Scenario summary, vulnerability framing, impact, and core testing purpose.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-APIT-04; WSTG title API Broken Function Level Authorization; WSTG category API Testing.\nBroken Function Level Authorization (BFLA) occurs when an API improperly enforces restrictions on users accessing certain functions or operations. This vulnerability allows attackers to invoke sensitive functions they are not authorized to execute, such as administrative functions or other high-privilege operations.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-APIT-04; WSTG title API Broken Function Level Authorization; WSTG category API Testing.\nBFLA commonly arises when APIs expose multiple route locations that serve different user roles (e.g., user vs. admin) but fail to restrict access to these functions based on the user's authorization level.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-APIT-04; WSTG title API Broken Function Level Authorization; WSTG category API Testing.\nExploiting BFLA can lead to serious consequences such as privilege escalation, unauthorized access to sensitive functions (e.g., administrative operations), or exposure of critical functionalities that should only be accessible to specific user roles.\n\n## Test Objectives\n\nPurpose: Explicit objectives a tester should satisfy for this WSTG scenario.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-APIT-04; WSTG title API Broken Function Level Authorization; WSTG category API Testing.\n- The goal of this test is to determine if the API enforces **role or privilege-based access control** to restrict users from accessing or executing functions they are not authorized to use. This ensures that function-level security boundaries are properly enforced.\n\n## Prerequisites And Environment\n\nPurpose: Target conditions, reachable inputs, side channels, and applicability constraints.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-APIT-04; WSTG title API Broken Function Level Authorization; WSTG category API Testing.\nLog in as a lower-privilege user (e.g., guest or regular user) and send requests to route locations that perform sensitive actions reserved for higher-privilege roles (e.g., admin).\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-APIT-04; WSTG title API Broken Function Level Authorization; WSTG category API Testing.\nIn **GraphQL APIs**, test if a user can invoke functions restricted to higher-privilege roles by modifying GraphQL queries.\n\n## Defenses And Detections\n\nPurpose: Controls, validation behavior, mitigations, detection signals, and defensive limits.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-APIT-04; WSTG title API Broken Function Level Authorization; WSTG category API Testing.\n- **Successful exploitation**: If a lower-privilege user (e.g., regular user or guest) can execute high-privilege functions or perform actions reserved for other roles (e.g., admin).\n- **Error responses**: Properly secured APIs in general would return `403 Forbidden` or `401 Unauthorized` when invoked restricted functions instead of a `200 OK` response.\n- **Inconsistent enforcement**: Some route locations enforce role-based restrictions while others do not, which indicates inconsistent security controls.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-APIT-04; WSTG title API Broken Function Level Authorization; WSTG category API Testing.\nTo prevent BFLA vulnerabilities, implement the following mitigations:\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-APIT-04; WSTG title API Broken Function Level Authorization; WSTG category API Testing.\n- **Enforce Role-Based Access Control (RBAC)**: Ensure that the API checks user roles and permissions at the **function level** before allowing access to certain operations. Only authorized roles should be allowed to invoke sensitive functions.\n- **Least Privilege Principle**: Apply the principle of least privilege by ensuring that users can only access the minimum set of functions they need for their role.\n- **Centralized Access Control Logic**: Use centralized access control logic to ensure consistency across all target API route locations. This avoids gaps where some functions may lack proper access checks.\n\n## Code And Payload Examples\n\nPurpose: Payloads, URLs, SQL snippets, command examples, and concrete request material.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-APIT-04; WSTG title API Broken Function Level Authorization; WSTG category API Testing.\n- **Administrative functions** (e.g., `/api/admin/deleteUser`, `/api/admin/getAllUsers`)\n- **Role-based operations** (e.g., `/api/admin/promoteUser`, `/api/user/createOrder`)\n- **Critical functions** for users (e.g., `/api/user/withdrawFunds`)"}
{"reference_id": "2", "content": "Anchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-03; WSTG title Testing for Privilege Escalation; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nPrivilege escalation occurs when a user gets access to more resources or functionality than they are normally allowed, and such elevation or changes should have been prevented by the application. This is usually caused by a flaw in the application. The result is that the application performs actions with more privileges than those intended by the developer or system administrator.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-03; WSTG title Testing for Privilege Escalation; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nThe degree of escalation depends on what privileges the attacker is authorized to possess, and what privileges can be obtained in a successful exploit. For example, a programming error that allows a user to gain extra privilege after successful authentication limits the degree of escalation, because the user is already authorized to hold some privilege. Likewise, a remote attacker gaining superuser privilege without any authentication presents a greater degree of escalation.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-03; WSTG title Testing for Privilege Escalation; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nUsually, people refer to *vertical escalation* when it is possible to access resources granted to more privileged accounts (e.g., acquiring administrative privileges for the application), and to *horizontal escalation* when it is possible to access resources granted to a similarly configured account (e.g., in an online banking application, accessing information related to a different user).\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-03; WSTG title Testing for Privilege Escalation; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\n- Identify injection points related to privilege manipulation.\n- Fuzz or otherwise attempt to bypass security measures.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-03; WSTG title Testing for Privilege Escalation; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nFor example:\nThe following HTTP POST allows the user that belongs to `grp001` to access order #0001:\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-03; WSTG title Testing for Privilege Escalation; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nVerify if a user that does not belong to `grp001` can modify the value of the request input fields `groupID` and `orderID` to gain access to that privileged data."}
{"reference_id": "2", "content": "Anchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-03; WSTG title Testing for Privilege Escalation; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nIn this condition, verify that it is not possible to escalate privileges by modifying the request input values. In this particular example, by modifying the `PVValid` value from `-1` to `0` (no error conditions), it may be possible to authenticate as administrator to the server.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-03; WSTG title Testing for Privilege Escalation; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\n```text\nX-Forwarded-For: 8.1.1.1\n```\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-03; WSTG title Testing for Privilege Escalation; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nIn this case, if the site uses the value of `X-forwarded-For` as client IP address, tester may change the IP value of the `X-forwarded-For` HTTP header to workaround the IP source identification.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-03; WSTG title Testing for Privilege Escalation; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nSuppose that the `deleteEvent` function is part of the administrator account menu of the application, and it is possible to access it by requesting the following URL: `<scheme>://<host>/<path>`. Then, the following HTTP request is generated when calling the `deleteEvent` function:\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-03; WSTG title Testing for Privilege Escalation; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\n```http\nPOST /account/deleteEvent HTTP/1.1\nHost: www.example.com\n[other HTTP headers]\nCookie: SessionID=ADMINISTRATOR_USER_SESSION\n\nEventID=1000001\n```\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-03; WSTG title Testing for Privilege Escalation; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\n```http\nHTTP/1.1 200 OK\n[other HTTP headers]\n\n{\"message\": \"Event was deleted\"}\n```\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-03; WSTG title Testing for Privilege Escalation; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\n```http\nPOST /account/deleteEvent HTTP/1.1\nHost: www.example.com\n[other HTTP headers]\nCookie: SessionID=CUSTOMER_USER_SESSION\n\nEventID=1000002\n```\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-03; WSTG title Testing for Privilege Escalation; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nIf the response of the attacker’s request contains the same data `{\"message\": \"Event was deleted\"}` the application is vulnerable.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-03; WSTG title Testing for Privilege Escalation; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\n```text\n/../.././userInfo.html\n```\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-03; WSTG title Testing for Privilege Escalation; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\n```text\nstartswith(), endswith(), contains(), indexOf()\n```\n\n## Canonical Relation Anchors\n\nPurpose: provide stable graph targets so multi-hop retrieval can resolve abstract web features to canonical WSTG test cases.\n\n- WSTG scenario WSTG-ATHZ-03 has title Testing for Privilege Escalation.\n- WSTG scenario WSTG-ATHZ-03 belongs to category Authorization Testing (ATHZ).\n- WSTG category Authorization Testing contains WSTG scenario WSTG-ATHZ-03.\n- Methodology for Testing for Privilege Escalation maps to WSTG scenario WSTG-ATHZ-03.\n- WSTG scenario WSTG-ATHZ-03 contains test objectives, attack methods, prerequisites, defenses, detections, payload examples, and observable signals.\n- Concept Testing for Privilege Escalation maps to WSTG scenario WSTG-ATHZ-03.\n- Concept Privilege Escalation maps to WSTG scenario WSTG-ATHZ-03.\n\n## Relation Briefs\n\nPurpose: preserve source-grounded operational claims connecting testing methods, target conditions, defensive behavior, weakness categories, and payload examples.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-03; WSTG title Testing for Privilege Escalation; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nThis section describes the issue of escalating privileges from one stage to another. During this phase, the tester should verify that it is not possible for a user to modify their privileges or roles inside the application in ways that could allow privilege escalation attacks.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-03; WSTG title Testing for Privilege Escalation; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nPrivilege escalation occurs when a user gets access to more resources or functionality than they are normally allowed, and such elevation or changes should have been prevented by the application. This is usually caused by a flaw in the application. The result is that the application performs actions with more privileges than those intended by the developer or system administrator."}
{"reference_id": "5", "content": "# Methodology Scenario\n\n## Methodology Scope\n\nThis document contains reusable web application testing methodology.\n\n## WSTG Scenario Anchor\n\n- WSTG ID: WSTG-ATHN-04\n- WSTG title: Testing for Bypassing Authentication Schema\n- WSTG category: Authentication Testing (ATHN)\n- Source file: data/lightrag/inputs/wstg_raw/4-Web_Application_Security_Testing/04-Authentication_Testing/04-Testing_for_Bypassing_Authentication_Schema.md\n- Canonical aliases: WSTG-ATHN-04, Testing for Bypassing Authentication Schema, Bypassing Authentication Schema, Authentication Testing, ATHN\n- Multi-hop retrieval target: features, technologies, vulnerability families, input vectors, payload patterns, observable signals, preconditions, and methodology steps in this document map back to WSTG-ATHN-04.\n\n## Overview\n\nPurpose: Scenario summary, vulnerability framing, impact, and core testing purpose.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHN-04; WSTG title Testing for Bypassing Authentication Schema; WSTG category Authentication Testing.\nAuthentication schema testing analyzes how the authentication process works and uses that information to test whether the authentication mechanism can be bypassed.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHN-04; WSTG title Testing for Bypassing Authentication Schema; WSTG category Authentication Testing.\nWhile most applications require authentication to gain access to private information or to execute tasks, not every authentication method is able to provide adequate security. Negligence, ignorance, or simple understatement of security threats often result in authentication schemes that can be bypassed by simply skipping the log in page and directly calling an internal page that is supposed to be accessed only after authentication has been performed.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHN-04; WSTG title Testing for Bypassing Authentication Schema; WSTG category Authentication Testing.\nIn addition, it is often possible to bypass authentication measures by tampering with requests and tricking the application into thinking that the user is already authenticated. This can be accomplished either by modifying the given URL parameter, by manipulating the form, or by counterfeiting sessions.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHN-04; WSTG title Testing for Bypassing Authentication Schema; WSTG category Authentication Testing.\nProblems related to the authentication schema can be found at different stages of the software development lifecycle (SDLC), like the design, development, and deployment phases:\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHN-04; WSTG title Testing for Bypassing Authentication Schema; WSTG category Authentication Testing.\n- In the design phase errors can include a wrong definition of application sections to be protected, the choice of not applying strong encryption protocols for securing the transmission of credentials, and many more.\n- In the development phase errors can include the incorrect implementation of input validation functionality or not following the security best practices for the specific language.\n- In the application deployment phase, there may be issues during the application setup (installation and configuration activities) due to a lack in required technical skills or due to the lack of good documentation.\n\n## Test Objectives\n\nPurpose: Explicit objectives a tester should satisfy for this WSTG scenario.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHN-04; WSTG title Testing for Bypassing Authentication Schema; WSTG category Authentication Testing.\n- Ensure that authentication is applied across all services that require it.\n\n## Attack Methods\n\nPurpose: Concrete testing procedures, probes, exploitation approaches, and technique variants.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHN-04; WSTG title Testing for Bypassing Authentication Schema; WSTG category Authentication Testing.\nThere are several methods of bypassing the authentication schema that is used by a web application:\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHN-04; WSTG title Testing for Bypassing Authentication Schema; WSTG category Authentication Testing.\n- Parameter modification\n- Session ID prediction\n- SQL injection\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHN-04; WSTG title Testing for Bypassing Authentication Schema; WSTG category Authentication Testing.\nIn the following figure, values inside cookies change only partially, so it's possible to restrict a brute force attack to the defined fields shown below.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHN-04; WSTG title Testing for Bypassing Authentication Schema; WSTG category Authentication Testing.\nSQL Injection is a widely known attack technique. This section is not going to describe this technique in detail as there are several sections in this guide that explain injection techniques beyond the scope of this section.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHN-04; WSTG title Testing for Bypassing Authentication Schema; WSTG category Authentication Testing.\nThe following figure shows that with a simple SQL injection attack, it is sometimes possible to bypass the authentication form.\n\n## Prerequisites And Environment\n\nPurpose: Target conditions, reachable inputs, side channels, and applicability constraints."}
{"reference_id": "6", "content": "# Methodology Scenario\n\n## Methodology Scope\n\nThis document contains reusable web application testing methodology.\n\n## WSTG Scenario Anchor\n\n- WSTG ID: WSTG-IDNT-01\n- WSTG title: Test Role Definitions\n- WSTG category: Identity Management Testing (IDNT)\n- Source file: data/lightrag/inputs/wstg_raw/4-Web_Application_Security_Testing/03-Identity_Management_Testing/01-Test_Role_Definitions.md\n- Canonical aliases: WSTG-IDNT-01, Test Role Definitions, Role Definitions, Identity Management Testing, IDNT\n- Multi-hop retrieval target: features, technologies, vulnerability families, input vectors, payload patterns, observable signals, preconditions, and methodology steps in this document map back to WSTG-IDNT-01.\n\n## Overview\n\nPurpose: Scenario summary, vulnerability framing, impact, and core testing purpose.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-IDNT-01; WSTG title Test Role Definitions; WSTG category Identity Management Testing.\nApplications have several types of functionalities and services, and those require access permissions based on the needs of the user. That user could be:\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-IDNT-01; WSTG title Test Role Definitions; WSTG category Identity Management Testing.\n- An administrator, where they manage the application functionalities.\n- An auditor, where they review the application transactions and provide a detailed report.\n- A support engineer, where they help customers debug and fix issues on their accounts.\n- A customer, where they interact with the application and benefit from its services.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-IDNT-01; WSTG title Test Role Definitions; WSTG category Identity Management Testing.\nIn order to handle these uses and any other use case for that application, role definitions are setup (more commonly known as RBAC). Based on these roles, the user is capable of accomplishing the required task.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-IDNT-01; WSTG title Test Role Definitions; WSTG category Identity Management Testing.\n> Note: Some applications define the roles of the user on creation, through rigorous checks and policies, or by ensuring that the user's role is properly protected through a signature created by the backend. Finding that roles exist doesn't mean that they're a vulnerability.\n\n## Test Objectives\n\nPurpose: Explicit objectives a tester should satisfy for this WSTG scenario.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-IDNT-01; WSTG title Test Role Definitions; WSTG category Identity Management Testing.\n- Identify and document roles used by the application.\n- Attempt to switch, change, or access another role.\n- Review the granularity of the roles and the needs behind the permissions given.\n\n## Attack Methods\n\nPurpose: Concrete testing procedures, probes, exploitation approaches, and technique variants.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-IDNT-01; WSTG title Test Role Definitions; WSTG category Identity Management Testing.\nAfter identifying possible attack vectors, the tester needs to test and validate that they can access the available roles.\n\n## Prerequisites And Environment\n\nPurpose: Target conditions, reachable inputs, side channels, and applicability constraints.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-IDNT-01; WSTG title Test Role Definitions; WSTG category Identity Management Testing.\nUsers should also not be able to perform actions that increase their own privileges (such as adding themselves to a more privileged role) outside of very specific circumstances.\n\n## Code And Payload Examples\n\nPurpose: Payloads, URLs, SQL snippets, command examples, and concrete request material.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-IDNT-01; WSTG title Test Role Definitions; WSTG category Identity Management Testing.\n- Application documentation.\n- Guidance by the developers or administrators of the application.\n- Application comments.\n- Fuzz possible roles:\n    - Cookie variable (*e.g.* `role=admin`, `isAdmin=True`)\n    - Account variable (*e.g.* `Role: manager`)\n    - Hidden directories or files (*e.g.* `/admin`, `/mod`, `/backups`)\n    - Switching to well known users (*e.g.* `admin`, `backups`, etc.)\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-IDNT-01; WSTG title Test Role Definitions; WSTG category Identity Management Testing.\nAn administrator shouldn't have full powers on the system. Sensitive admin functionality should leverage a maker-checker principle, or use MFA to ensure that the administrator is conducting the transaction. A clear example on this was the Twitter incident in 2020.\n\n## Canonical Relation Anchors\n\nPurpose: provide stable graph targets so multi-hop retrieval can resolve abstract web features to canonical WSTG test cases.\n\n- WSTG scenario WSTG-IDNT-01 has title Test Role Definitions.\n- WSTG scenario WSTG-IDNT-01 belongs to category Identity Management Testing (IDNT).\n- WSTG category Identity Management Testing contains WSTG scenario WSTG-IDNT-01.\n- Methodology for Test Role Definitions maps to WSTG scenario WSTG-IDNT-01.\n- WSTG scenario WSTG-IDNT-01 contains test objectives, attack methods, prerequisites, defenses, detections, payload examples, and observable signals.\n- Concept Test Role Definitions maps to WSTG scenario WSTG-IDNT-01.\n- Concept Role Definitions maps to WSTG scenario WSTG-IDNT-01.\n\n## Relation Briefs\n\nPurpose: preserve source-grounded operational claims connecting testing methods, target conditions, defensive behavior, weakness categories, and payload examples."}
