{"entity": "Accessible Upload Handler", "type": "PreconditionEnvironment", "description": "When the web application provides a directory or endpoint where files can be uploaded for resources such as user avatars or themes.<SEP>Accessible Upload Handler is a web endpoint or component configured to accept and process user-uploaded files or data through form interfaces or direct API calls, saving content to the server's file system for later web access. While fulfilling an essential user-facing function for content submission such as images and documents, these handlers become significant security risks when inadequately secured. Exposed configurations lacking authentication, permission checks, or input validation allow attackers — whether unauthenticated or authenticated — to interact with the handler. This vulnerability serves as a critical attack vector, frequently enabling malicious actors to upload and install webshells, execute remote code, exfiltrate data, or fully compromise the server. Consequently, an Accessible Upload Handler describes a target condition where a reachable upload endpoint accepts user-supplied data without proper validation or extension controls, permitting the unauthorized storage of malicious artifacts on the system.<SEP>A web endpoint or interface that allows receiving, storing, or processing uploaded files from unauthenticated or low-privilege users.<SEP>A web endpoint or functionality that accepts and processes user-uploaded files.<SEP>A target file-upload component reachable via API, web form or service, subject to insufficient validation.<SEP>A target component or endpoint that accepts and processes user-uploaded files without adequate validation.<SEP>An upload endpoint accessible to an attacker that lacks sufficient restriction on file type or size."}
