{"entity": "Privilege Escalation Path", "type": "AttackerCapability", "description": "Provides steps to reach higher permissions by pivoting through systems authenticated by cracked credentials.<SEP>Privilege Escalation Path refers to a reusable sequence of steps, techniques, or conditions that allow an attacker to progress from lower system privileges to higher privileges or full control of a target system. It represents a feasible chain of exploitable weaknesses, misconfigurations, or trusted relationships that together facilitate unauthorized access elevation. Often serving as a pivot point following initial compromise—for example via web shells in Windows Active Directory environments—this pathway underpins broader attacks aimed at achieving domain-wide control or root access. The term describes a strategic objective to increase an attacker's effective permissions beyond their initial constrained role, moving toward administrative or root-level access within the target environment. A Privilege Escalation Path constitutes a sequence of actions, discovered conditions, or interconnected vulnerabilities that permit the systematic accumulation of higher-level system permissions after initial access is obtained. This sequence enables lateral movement, expanded operational ability, and ultimately grants the user permissions more extensive than originally permitted, thereby facilitating unauthorized control. Acquired credentials such as valid user hashes further support this progression by allowing the attacker to authenticate and perform subsequent privilege escalation steps when encountering accounts with higher scopes or admin roles.<SEP>A pre-existing method or sequence of steps the attacker can follow to elevate their permissions from an initial low-privilege context to SYSTEM or root level.<SEP>A sequence of vulnerabilities or permission misconfigurations that can lead to gaining higher administrative privileges.<SEP>A sequence of actions or existing session states that lead toward gaining higher administrative or system access rights.<SEP>A strategic result where an attacker moves up system permissions to gain administrative or root-level access.<SEP>A chain of exploits allowing attacker access to increase permissions to root or admin level."}
{"entity": "Server-Side Request Forgery", "type": "VulnerabilityClass", "description": "A weakness class in which the server makes unauthorized outbound requests using attacker-controlled parameters.<SEP>An attack technique where an application is induced to make requests to remote or internal backend services.<SEP>A weakness where server-side components fetch remote URLs specified by the user, allowing attacker-controlled requests to internal or external systems.<SEP>A weakness where the server performs unexpected outbound requests to attacker-controlled or trusted IP addresses based on user input."}
{"entity": "Credentials", "type": "AttackerCapability", "description": "Credentials are reusable authentication secrets such as usernames, passwords, API keys, session tokens, or similarly valid authentication data that grant or assert access to systems or data. Functionally, credentials serve as the foundational artifacts enabling unauthorized access by authenticating as legitimate users or services. These secrets can exist in plaintext, hashed form, or other usable states and are frequently sought after during reconnaissance or exploitation phases. Credentials include usernames paired with their associated passwords or hashes, API keys, session identifiers, cookies, tokens, or other login information. Once acquired through theft, harvesting, extraction from configuration files, or password cracking, these details provide attackers with the means to escalate privileges and pivot within a compromised network. Organizations face significant risk when credentials are stolen or exposed, as they become critical inputs for subsequent attack steps including account impersonation and follow-up malicious actions.<SEP>Stolen usernames, passwords, API keys, or session tokens collected during the attack for reuse or post-exploitation access.<SEP>A set of authentication tokens, keys, or secrets extracted from the target system and reusable later in the attack.<SEP>Stolen or cracked usernames and hashed or plaintext passwords obtained via attacks."}
{"entity": "URL Userinfo Confusion Payload", "type": "PayloadPattern", "description": "URL Userinfo Confusion Payload is a reusable, parameterized malicious input technique designed to exploit variations in URL userinfo encoding to bypass access controls, authentication mechanisms, or filtering logic. It takes the form of a malformed or ambiguous string embedded within URL path or query components, intentionally confusing the parsing of hostname/userinfo segments. By probing defenses against misinterpreted userinfo parameters, this technique exploits inconsistencies in URL handler implementations to evade authorization checks, proxy filters, or authentication validation. Common targets include userinfo and query parameters, crafted with ambiguous syntax to disrupt strict adherence to URL structure interpretation. Functionally, it serves both as a malicious input probe to circumvent security mechanisms and as a vehicle for testing bypass techniques such as path traversal, directory listing circumvention, or injecting unauthorized parameters through URL encoding manipulation or parameter injection ambiguities. Ultimately, it is a malformed URL parameter or parameter injection pattern leveraging userinfo syntax variations to confuse server-side user info resolution and authentication validation, often aiming to expose authenticated session tokens, trick authentication flows, or exploit gaps in input sanitization and URL routing logic.<SEP>Reusable parameterized malicious input designed to confuse or bypass URL authentication mechanisms by manipulating userinfo components in the request path.<SEP>A parameter substitution pattern that misconfigured applications treat as path information rather than authentication data.<SEP>A reusable malicious input pattern that manipulates URL userinfo to confuse server-side authorization logic.<SEP>A parameterized or modified URL userinfo segment designed to confuse or bypass input validation mechanisms."}
{"entity": "Credentialed Login Access", "type": "AttackerCapability", "description": "Reusable access using valid credentials issued by the target system, enabling session manipulation and privilege abuse."}
{"entity": "Offline Password Cracking", "type": "AttackTechnique", "description": "Offline Password Cracking refers to a cybersecurity threat wherein attackers recover plaintext passwords from compromised hash values stored locally, without direct network interaction. This technique employs brute-force or dictionary attacks against pre-intercepted credentials that have been stolen via external breaches, exfiltrated data, or post-exploitation activities. Attackers utilize local computational resources to systematically compare guessed passwords against hash results, often leveraging precomputed wordlists, custom rules, or exhaustive password combinations to uncover valid credentials. Key strategies include comparing extensive dictionaries with captured hashes or iterating through all possible password combinations to find matches. While it serves as a valid offensive security testing methodology to evaluate the strength of hashing and password policies, it is frequently employed by adversaries to gain unauthorized system access by validating cracked credentials against harvested datasets. This poses a significant security risk to systems utilizing weak, predictable, or reused passwords, underscoring the necessity of robust password policies, computationally intensive hashing algorithms like bcrypt, and mitigation controls such as account lockouts or rate limiting to defend against such attacks.<SEP>A technique used to recover password hashes through computational methods without live network access.<SEP>A technique that uses local computational resources to defeat weak authentication controls without direct network contact.<SEP>An action that uses computational tools to attempt to discover plaintext passwords from hashed credentials conducted outside the target system.<SEP>Technique using dictionary or brute-force attacks on password hashes obtained via credential exposure or hashed credentials leaked through verbose errors.<SEP>Brute-force or dictionary attack on ca