{"entity1": "JWT Authentication Flow", "entity2": "JWT Token Forgery", "description": "JWT Authentication Flow and JWT Token Forgery is a security attack that targets the validation phase of the JSON Web Token (JWT) authentication lifecycle, leveraging vulnerabilities in token verification logic to bypass authorization checks and enable unauthorized access or privilege escalation. This technique abuses systems that depend on standard JWT-based mechanisms for session management and authentication by forging or manipulating tokens to deceive authentication validators. Occurring only when applications actively employ JWT tokens for authentication, the attack specifically exploits weaknesses in implementations that fail to enforce strict verification of valid signatures, token expiration, and access control policies. Consequently, securing the entire JWT lifecycle—particularly the token validation steps—remains critical for preventing successful forgery and maintaining application integrity, as attackers rely on manipulating claims or signing processes to circumvent expected authorization behavior and bypass JWT-based protection mechanisms.<SEP>This technique focuses on attacking the token generation or validation process within the JWT session flow.<SEP>The JWT authentication flow becomes the mechanism that an attacker aims to forge or bypass tokens."}
