{"entity": "PHP", "type": "TechnologyStack", "description": "A web application environment based on the PHP programming language often with associated frameworks and databases.<SEP>PHP is a server-side scripting language widely used for web development to generate dynamic web pages and handle backend logic. It executes server-side code to produce web pages with dynamic behavior such as database interactions, session management, and user input handling. Due to its ubiquity, PHP applications and interpreters frequently become targets for web shell uploads and alternate extension bypasses. Security assessments often focus on PHP code to identify insecure features like improper file uploads, command execution vulnerabilities, and potential exploits through insecure deserialization of user-provided data. While it remains a foundational technology for many web applications, its prevalence also makes it a common vector for injection and execution flaws, necessitating robust security practices during development and deployment.<SEP>A server-side scripting language used in web application development and relevant to the observed attack techniques.<SEP>A server-side scripting language commonly used for web applications, identifiable as the target backend technology.<SEP>Server-side scripting language commonly used in web applications and under examination for vulnerabilities.<SEP>A server-side scripting language commonly associated with web applications and injections.<SEP>A web scripting language often used for dynamic server-side application development, targeted by various web attacks.<SEP>A popular server-side scripting language used for dynamic web page generation and backend logic."}
{"entity": "URL Userinfo Confusion Payload", "type": "PayloadPattern", "description": "URL Userinfo Confusion Payload is a reusable, parameterized malicious input technique designed to exploit variations in URL userinfo encoding to bypass access controls, authentication mechanisms, or filtering logic. It takes the form of a malformed or ambiguous string embedded within URL path or query components, intentionally confusing the parsing of hostname/userinfo segments. By probing defenses against misinterpreted userinfo parameters, this technique exploits inconsistencies in URL handler implementations to evade authorization checks, proxy filters, or authentication validation. Common targets include userinfo and query parameters, crafted with ambiguous syntax to disrupt strict adherence to URL structure interpretation. Functionally, it serves both as a malicious input probe to circumvent security mechanisms and as a vehicle for testing bypass techniques such as path traversal, directory listing circumvention, or injecting unauthorized parameters through URL encoding manipulation or parameter injection ambiguities. Ultimately, it is a malformed URL parameter or parameter injection pattern leveraging userinfo syntax variations to confuse server-side user info resolution and authentication validation, often aiming to expose authenticated session tokens, trick authentication flows, or exploit gaps in input sanitization and URL routing logic.<SEP>Reusable parameterized malicious input designed to confuse or bypass URL authentication mechanisms by manipulating userinfo components in the request path.<SEP>A parameter substitution pattern that misconfigured applications treat as path information rather than authentication data.<SEP>A reusable malicious input pattern that manipulates URL userinfo to confuse server-side authorization logic.<SEP>A parameterized or modified URL userinfo segment designed to confuse or bypass input validation mechanisms."}
{"entity": "Source Code Review", "type": "AttackTechnique", "description": "Source Code Review is a systematic technique for analyzing source code—typically from public repositories, exposed APIs, or extracted builds—to identify security vulnerabilities, sensitive information disclosures, and configuration weaknesses. It involves methodical scrutiny of code files to detect hard-coded credentials, misconfigurations, improper trust boundaries, injection points, and insecure implementation flaws. This process allows organizations to uncover logic weaknesses, hidden capabilities, and exploitable components before they can be leveraged by attackers. By extracting configuration data, secrets, and identifying potential attack vectors such as privilege escalation or sensitive data leaks, source code review informs risk mitigation strategies and strengthens the overall security posture of software systems. It is employed both defensively by security teams to remediate issues proactively and offensively by threat actors to map potential exploitation paths, making it a critical component in application security testing and software development lifecycle security practices.<SEP>A testing activity analyzing accessible source code repositories to discover hardcoded credentials, logic flaws, or path traversal opportunities."}
