{"reference_id": "1", "content": "Anchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-1; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nThe AS responds with a redirect containing the authorization code. This can be exchanged with an access token in the token request. As shown below, the URL in the `Location` header is the URI given in the previous `redirect_uri` parameter.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-1; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\n```http\nHTTP/1.1 302 Found\nDate: Mon, 18 Oct 2021 20:46:44 GMT\nContent-Type: text/html; charset=utf-8\nContent-Length: 340\nLocation: <scheme>://<host>/<path>\n```\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-1; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\n1. Start the OAuth flow and pause it at the authorization request.\n2. Change the value of the `redirect_uri` and observe the response.\n3. Investigate the response and identify if the arbitrary `redirect_uri` parameter was accepted by the AS.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-1; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nIf the AS redirects the user agent to the `redirect_uri` you specified, the AS does not properly validate the `redirect_uri`.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-1; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nDuring the Authorization Code flow code exchange, a code is issued by the AS to the client and later exchanged against the token route location to retrieve an authorization token and a refresh token.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-1; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\n1. Send a valid code for another `client_id`.\n2. Send a valid code for another resource owner.\n3. Send a valid code for another `redirect_uri`.\n4. Resend the code more than once (code replay).\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-1; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nThe request sent to the token route location contains the authorization code. It is exchanged against the token. Capture this request with an HTTP request capture workflow like request capture tooling and resend the request with modified values.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-1; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\n```http\nPOST /oauth/token HTTP/1.1\nHost: as.example.com\n[...]\n\n{\n    \"errorPath\":\"/error\",\n    \"client_id\":\"example-client\",\n    \"code\":\"INJECT_CODE_HERE\",\n    \"grant_type\":\"authorization_code\",\n    \"redirect_uri\":\"<scheme>://<host>/<path>\n}\n```\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-1; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nIf the AS responds with an `access_token`, the code was successfully injected.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-1; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nAs the OAuth flow for confidential clients is additionally protected by a client secret, it is not possible to directly submit an authorization code to the token route location. Instead, inject the authorization code into the client. This injected code will then be sent in the token request, issued by the confidential client together with the client secret.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-1; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nFirst, capture an authorization code from the AS:\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-1; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\n1. Start the authorization code flow with user Alice. Pause when you receive a code from the AS.\n2. Do not submit the code to the client and keep note of the code and corresponding state.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-1; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nThen, inject the code:\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-1; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\n1. Start the authorization code flow with user Mallory and inject the previously gathered code and state values for user Alice into the process.\n 2. When the attack is successful, the client should now be in possession of an `authorization_token` that grants access to resources owned by user Alice.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-1; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\n```http\nGET /callback?code=INJECT_CODE_HERE&state=example HTTP/1.1\nHost: client.example.com\n[...]\n\n```"}
{"reference_id": "2", "content": "# Methodology Scenario\n\n## Methodology Scope\n\nThis document contains reusable web application testing methodology.\n\n## WSTG Scenario Anchor\n\n- WSTG ID: WSTG-ATHZ-05\n- WSTG title: Testing for OAuth Weaknesses\n- WSTG category: Authorization Testing (ATHZ)\n- Source file: data/lightrag/inputs/wstg_raw/4-Web_Application_Security_Testing/05-Authorization_Testing/05-Testing_for_OAuth_Weaknesses.md\n- Canonical aliases: WSTG-ATHZ-05, Testing for OAuth Weaknesses, OAuth Weaknesses, Authorization Testing, ATHZ\n- Multi-hop retrieval target: features, technologies, vulnerability families, input vectors, payload patterns, observable signals, preconditions, and methodology steps in this document map back to WSTG-ATHZ-05.\n\n## Ontology Query Anchors\n\nPurpose: Phase 2 ontology anchors.\n\n- PreconditionEnvironment: Sequential Object Identifier, Adjacent Account ID Request\n- VulnerabilityClass: Insecure Direct Object Reference, Broken Object-Level Authorization\n- AttackTechnique: Object ID Tampering\n- ObservableSignal: Cross-Tenant Object Access\n- Retrieval link: matching profile evidence should retrieve WSTG scenario WSTG-ATHZ-05.\n\n## Overview\n\nPurpose: Scenario summary, vulnerability framing, impact, and core testing purpose.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nOAuth2.0 (hereinafter referred to as OAuth) is an authorization framework that allows a client to access resources on the behalf of its user.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nIn order to achieve this, OAuth heavily relies on tokens to communicate between the different entities, each entity having a different role:\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\n- **Resource Owner:** The entity who grants access to a resource, the owner, and in most cases is the user themselves\n- **Client:** The application that is requesting access to a resource on behalf of the Resource Owner. These clients come in two types:\n    - **Public:** clients that can't protect a secret (*e.g.* frontend focused applications, such as SPAs, mobile applications, etc.)\n    - **Confidential:** clients that are able to securely authenticate with the authorization server by keeping their registered secrets safe (*e.g.* backend services)\n- **Authorization Server:** The server that holds authorization information and grants the access\n- **Resource Server:** The application that serves the content accessed by the client\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nSince OAuth's responsibility is to delegate access rights by the owner to the client, this is a very attractive target for attackers, and bad implementations lead to unauthorized access to the users' resources and information.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nIn order to provide access to a client application, OAuth relies on several authorization grant types to generate an access token:\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\n- Authorization Code: used by both confidential and public clients to exchange an authorization code for an access token, but recommended only for confidential clients\n- Proof Key for Code Exchange (PKCE): PKCE builds on top of the Authorization Code grant, providing stronger security for it to be used by public clients, and improving the posture of confidential ones\n- Client Credentials: used for machine to machine communication, where the \"user\" here is the machine requesting access to its own resources from the Resource Server\n- Device Code: used for devices with limited input capabilities.\n- Refresh Token: tokens provided by the authorization server to allow clients to refresh users' access tokens once they become invalid or expire. This grant type is used in conjunction with one other grant type.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nTwo flows will be deprecated in the release of OAuth2.1, and their usage is not recommended:\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\n- Implicit Flow*: PKCE's secure implementation renders this flow obsolete. Prior to PKCE, the implicit flow was used by client-side applications such as single page applications since CORS relaxed the same-origin policy for sites to inter-communicate. For more information on why the implicit grant is not recommended, review this section.\n- Resource Owner Password Credentials:used to exchange users' credentials directly with the client, which then sends them to the authorization to exchange them for an access token. For information on why this flow is not recommended, review this section."}
{"reference_id": "1", "content": "# Methodology Scenario\n\n## Methodology Scope\n\nThis document contains reusable web application testing methodology.\n\n## WSTG Scenario Anchor\n\n- WSTG ID: WSTG-ATHZ-05-1\n- WSTG title: Testing for OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses\n- WSTG category: Authorization Testing (ATHZ)\n- Source file: data/lightrag/inputs/wstg_raw/4-Web_Application_Security_Testing/05-Authorization_Testing/05.1-Testing_for_OAuth_Authorization_Server_Weaknesses.md\n- Canonical aliases: WSTG-ATHZ-05-1, Testing for OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses, OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses, Authorization Testing, ATHZ\n- Multi-hop retrieval target: features, technologies, vulnerability families, input vectors, payload patterns, observable signals, preconditions, and methodology steps in this document map back to WSTG-ATHZ-05-1.\n\n## Overview\n\nPurpose: Scenario summary, vulnerability framing, impact, and core testing purpose.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-1; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nOAuth stores the identities of users and their corresponding access rights with the Authorization Server (AS). The AS plays a crucial role during the OAuth flow as it grants clients access to resources. To be able to do that securely, it must properly validate request input fields that are part of the OAuth flow.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-1; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nFailure to validate the request input fields may lead to account takeover, unauthorized resource access and the elevation of privileges.\n\n## Test Objectives\n\nPurpose: Explicit objectives a tester should satisfy for this WSTG scenario.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-1; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\n- Identify weaknesses in the Authorization Server.\n\n## Attack Methods\n\nPurpose: Concrete testing procedures, probes, exploitation approaches, and technique variants.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-1; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nIn order to test for AS weaknesses, you will aim to:\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-1; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\n1. Retrieve credentials used for authorization.\n2. Grant yourself access to arbitrary resources through forced browsing.\n3. Bypass the authorization.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-1; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nIf a user opens this link in the user agent, the AS will redirect the user agent to the malicious URL.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-1; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nConduct the following tests against the AS:\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-1; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nThis can happen when:\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-1; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\n- The AS does not support PKCE.\n- The AS does not properly validate PKCE.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-1; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nBoth can be tested with an HTTP request capture workflow like request capture tooling. Conduct the following tests:\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-1; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nThe example below highlights the values to modify:\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-1; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nCSRF attacks are described in CSRF. OAuth can be attacked with CSRF.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-1; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nTest every request that contains one of the anti-CSRF request input fields used by OAuth according to the tests described in the CSRF test cases.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-1; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nThe consent page is displayed to a user to verify that this user consents in the client accessing the resource on the users behalf. Attacking the consent page with CSRF may grant an arbitrary client access to a resource on behalf of the user. The steps of this flow are:\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-1; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\n1. The Client generates a state parameter and sends it with the consent request.\n2. The User Agent displays the consent page.\n3. The Resource Owner grants access to the Client.\n4. The consent is sent to the AS together with the acknowledged scopes."}
{"reference_id": "1", "content": "```\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-1; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\n```http\nPOST /token HTTP/1.1\nHost: as.example.com\nCookie: [...]\n[...]\n\ngrant_type=refresh_token\n&refresh_token=eyJhbGciOiJIUz[...]\n&client_id=example-client\n\n```\n\n## Canonical Relation Anchors\n\nPurpose: provide stable graph targets so multi-hop retrieval can resolve abstract web features to canonical WSTG test cases.\n\n- WSTG scenario WSTG-ATHZ-05-1 has title Testing for OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses.\n- WSTG scenario WSTG-ATHZ-05-1 belongs to category Authorization Testing (ATHZ).\n- WSTG category Authorization Testing contains WSTG scenario WSTG-ATHZ-05-1.\n- Methodology for Testing for OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses maps to WSTG scenario WSTG-ATHZ-05-1.\n- WSTG scenario WSTG-ATHZ-05-1 contains test objectives, attack methods, prerequisites, defenses, detections, payload examples, and observable signals.\n- Concept Testing for OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses maps to WSTG scenario WSTG-ATHZ-05-1.\n- Concept OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses maps to WSTG scenario WSTG-ATHZ-05-1.\n\n## Relation Briefs\n\nPurpose: preserve source-grounded operational claims connecting testing methods, target conditions, defensive behavior, weakness categories, and payload examples.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-1; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nOAuth stores the identities of users and their corresponding access rights with the Authorization Server (AS). The AS plays a crucial role during the OAuth flow as it grants clients access to resources. To be able to do that securely, it must properly validate request input fields that are part of the OAuth flow.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-1; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nFailure to validate the request input fields may lead to account takeover, unauthorized resource access and the elevation of privileges.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-1; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\n- Identify weaknesses in the Authorization Server.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-1; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nIn order to test for AS weaknesses, you will aim to:\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-1; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\n1. Retrieve credentials used for authorization.\n2. Grant yourself access to arbitrary resources through forced browsing.\n3. Bypass the authorization.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-1; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nAdditionally, see the `Common Filter Bypass` section in Testing for Server-Side Request Forgery to identity common bypasses for redirect URI validation.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-1; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nAdditional measures can prevent CSRF attacks as well. The PKCE flow mitigates CSRF. A `nonce` value may act as an anti-CSRF token as well.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-1; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nFor this attack to be successful, the attacker needs to load the authorization page in an iframe.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-1; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nThe following HTML page can be used to load the authorization page in an iframe:\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-1; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nIf successfully loaded, the site is vulnerable to clickjacking.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-1; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nSee Testing for Clickjacking for a detailed description of how such an attack can be conducted.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-1; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nMost of the attacks against OAuth AS can be mitigated by validating the existence and content of request input fields during the authorization code and token exchange.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-1; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nRestrict the time span and allowed usage for credentials such as the authorization code and refresh token. This can mitigate some types of attacks and also limits the use of such credentials for an attacker, if they are gained."}
{"reference_id": "1", "content": "Anchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-1; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\n1. The Client generates a state parameter and sends it with the consent request.\n2. The User Agent displays the consent page.\n3. The Resource Owner grants access to the Client.\n4. The consent is sent to the AS together with the acknowledged scopes.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-1; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nUse an HTTP request capture workflow like request capture tooling to test whether the state parameter is properly validated.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-1; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nFor this attack to be successful, the attacker needs to load the authorization page in an iframe.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-1; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nThe following HTML page can be used to load the authorization page in an iframe:\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-1; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nIf successfully loaded, the site is vulnerable to clickjacking.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-1; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nSee Testing for Clickjacking for a detailed description of how such an attack can be conducted.\n\n## Prerequisites And Environment\n\nPurpose: Target conditions, reachable inputs, side channels, and applicability constraints.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-1; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nWhen a JSON Web Token (JWT) is used as the access token, it is possible to retrieve the validity of the access token from the decoded JWT. This is described in Testing JSON Web Tokens. It is possible that the AS does not properly validate the lifetime of the JWT.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-1; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nThis process can be optimized by automating the steps and logging of the server's response. When a response of HTTP status 403 (instead of HTTP status 200) is received, this can indicate that the access token is no longer valid.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-1; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\n1. Send the refresh token and determine if the AS hands out an access token.\n2. Repeat the steps with the same refresh token to evaluate how often a single refresh token is accepted.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-1; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nWhen a JWT is used as the refresh token, it is possible to retrieve the validity of the refresh token from the decoded JWT. This is described in Testing JSON Web Tokens. The refresh token may be valid for a longer period of time, but should have an expiry date.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-1; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\n1. Send the refresh token and determine if the AS hands out an access token.\n2. Repeat the steps with the same refresh token until it is invalidated.\n3. Use the refresh token from the last token response\n\n## Defenses And Detections\n\nPurpose: Controls, validation behavior, mitigations, detection signals, and defensive limits.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-1; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nTo test if the AS is vulnerable to insufficient redirect URI validation, capture the traffic with an HTTP request capture workflow such as request capture tooling.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-1; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nAdditionally, see the `Common Filter Bypass` section in Testing for Server-Side Request Forgery to identity common bypasses for redirect URI validation.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-1; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nAdditional measures can prevent CSRF attacks as well. The PKCE flow mitigates CSRF. A `nonce` value may act as an anti-CSRF token as well.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-1; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nTest for lifetime validation by sending the request after varying lengths of time have passed, for example, after 5 minutes, 10 minutes, and 30 minutes."}
{"reference_id": "1", "content": "Anchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-1; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nTest for lifetime validation by sending the request after varying lengths of time have passed, for example, after 5 minutes, 10 minutes, and 30 minutes.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-1; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nAdditional security can be gained with a theft detection mechanism. If a refresh token is used in a token exchange beyond its validity (or lifetime), the AS invalidates all refresh tokens. To test this mechanism:\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-1; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nIf all refresh tokens that were issued to the client for this resource owner are invalidated, the AS has token theft detection.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-1; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\n- Testing for Cross Site Request Forgery\n- Testing for Client-side URL Redirect\n- Testing for Server-Side Request Forgery\n- Testing JSON Web Tokens\n- Testing for Clickjacking\n- Testing Cross Origin Resource Sharing\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-1; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nMost of the attacks against OAuth AS can be mitigated by validating the existence and content of request input fields during the authorization code and token exchange.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-1; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nRestrict the time span and allowed usage for credentials such as the authorization code and refresh token. This can mitigate some types of attacks and also limits the use of such credentials for an attacker, if they are gained.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-1; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nProper configuration of security mitigation like CORS, anti-CSRF tokens, and anti-clickjacking headers can mitigate or limit the impact of attacks.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-1; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\n- Always validate if all request input fields are present, and validate their values.\n- Use the PKCE extension to properly secure the authorization code and token exchange.\n- Do not allow fallback for security features like the PKCE extension.\n- Restrict the lifetime of credentials.\n- Use credentials only once where possible, e.g. the authorization code.\n- Configure available security mitigation like CORS, anti-CSRF tokens, and anti-clickjacking headers.\n\n## Code And Payload Examples\n\nPurpose: Payloads, URLs, SQL snippets, command examples, and concrete request material.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-1; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nIf the `redirect_uri` is not properly validated, a link can be crafted that contains a URL pointing to a server controlled by an attacker. This can be used to trick the AS into sending an authorization code to the attacker. In the following example, `client.evil.com` is used as the forged `redirect_uri`.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-1; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\n```text\n<scheme>://<host>/<path>\n```\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-1; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nAn attacker can capture the `code` value passed in the spoofed URL and then submit it to the AS token route location.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-1; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nThe following request illustrates an authorization request that sends the `redirect_uri` to the authorization server. The client `client.example.com` sends an authorization request to the AS `as.example.com` with the URL-encoded redirect URI `http%3A%2F%2Fclient.example.com%2F`.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-1; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\n```http\nGET /authorize\n    ?redirect_uri=http%3A%2F%2Fclient.example.com%2F\n    &client_id=example-client\n    &errorPath=%2Ferror\n    &scope=openid%20profile%20email\n    &response_type=code\n    &response_mode=query\n    &state=example\n    &nonce=example\n    &code_challenge=example\n    &code_challenge_method=S256 HTTP/1.1\nHost: as.example.com\n```"}
{"reference_id": "3", "content": "# Methodology Scenario\n\n## Methodology Scope\n\nThis document contains reusable web application testing methodology.\n\n## WSTG Scenario Anchor\n\n- WSTG ID: WSTG-ATHZ-05-2\n- WSTG title: Testing for OAuth Client Weaknesses\n- WSTG category: Authorization Testing (ATHZ)\n- Source file: data/lightrag/inputs/wstg_raw/4-Web_Application_Security_Testing/05-Authorization_Testing/05.2-Testing_for_OAuth_Client_Weaknesses.md\n- Canonical aliases: WSTG-ATHZ-05-2, Testing for OAuth Client Weaknesses, OAuth Client Weaknesses, Authorization Testing, ATHZ\n- Multi-hop retrieval target: features, technologies, vulnerability families, input vectors, payload patterns, observable signals, preconditions, and methodology steps in this document map back to WSTG-ATHZ-05-2.\n\n## Overview\n\nPurpose: Scenario summary, vulnerability framing, impact, and core testing purpose.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-2; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Client Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nOAuth grants access rights on resources to clients. This allows them to act on behalf of the resource owner. The client receives the authorization code and refresh token in the token exchange and stores them.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-2; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Client Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nFailure to protect the token exchange and credentials may result in unauthorized resource access and the elevation of privileges.\n\n## Test Objectives\n\nPurpose: Explicit objectives a tester should satisfy for this WSTG scenario.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-2; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Client Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\n- Identify weaknesses in the OAuth client.\n\n## Attack Methods\n\nPurpose: Concrete testing procedures, probes, exploitation approaches, and technique variants.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-2; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Client Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nIn order to test for client weaknesses, you will aim to:\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-2; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Client Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\n1. Retrieve credentials used for authorization.\n2. Grant yourself access to arbitrary resources through forced browsing.\n3. Bypass the authorization.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-2; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Client Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nThe client secret is used to authenticate the client against the Authorization Server (AS) in order to prove that the client is a trusted origin.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-2; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Client Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nPublic clients are generally not able to store the client secret securely.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-2; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Client Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nIf this is not successful, you can also:\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-2; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Client Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nThe client receives access tokens and ideally stores them in a location where those tokens can be protected from attackers.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-2; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Client Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nConfidential clients should store tokens in volatile memory to prevent access through other attacks such as local file inclusion, attackers who are able to access the environment, or SQL Injection attacks.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-2; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Client Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nPublic clients, such as single-page applications, do not have the possibility of storing tokens securely. For example, a cross-site scripting attack allows attackers to access credentials stored in the browser.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-2; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Client Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nPublic clients may store tokens in the browsers session storage or in a cookie, but not in the local storage. To determine if tokens are improperly stored:\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-2; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Client Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\n1. Browse to the application.\n2. Retrieve an access token.\n3. Open the browser's developer tools.\n4. Navigate to the Application Tab.\n5. Locate the Local Storage and view stored data.\n6. Locate the Session Storage and view stored data.\n7. Locate the Cookie Store and view stored data.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-2; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Client Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nThis attack is only possible when the client uses a response type that directly issues an access token to the client. This occurs with the grant types Implicit Flows, Resource Owner Password Credential, and machine-to-machine flows. See Testing for OAuth Weaknesses for further description."}
{"reference_id": "2", "content": "Anchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nThe risk that's carried by the implicit flow leaking the tokens is far higher than leaking the `code` or any other `code_*` request input fields, as they are bound to specific clients and are harder to abuse in case of leakage.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nAfter stepping through the OAuth flow and using the application, a few requests are captured in the request history of an HTTP request capture workflow. Search for the HTTP referrer header (e.g. `Referer: <scheme>://<host>/<path>`) containing the authorization server and client URL in the request history.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nReviewing the HTML meta tags (although this tag is not supported on all browsers), or the Referrer-Policy could help assess if any credential leakage is happening through the referrer header.\n\n## Canonical Relation Anchors\n\nPurpose: provide stable graph targets so multi-hop retrieval can resolve abstract web features to canonical WSTG test cases.\n\n- WSTG scenario WSTG-ATHZ-05 has title Testing for OAuth Weaknesses.\n- WSTG scenario WSTG-ATHZ-05 belongs to category Authorization Testing (ATHZ).\n- WSTG category Authorization Testing contains WSTG scenario WSTG-ATHZ-05.\n- Methodology for Testing for OAuth Weaknesses maps to WSTG scenario WSTG-ATHZ-05.\n- WSTG scenario WSTG-ATHZ-05 contains test objectives, attack methods, prerequisites, defenses, detections, payload examples, and observable signals.\n- Concept Testing for OAuth Weaknesses maps to WSTG scenario WSTG-ATHZ-05.\n- Concept OAuth Weaknesses maps to WSTG scenario WSTG-ATHZ-05.\n- PreconditionEnvironment anchors Sequential Object Identifier, Adjacent Account ID Request map to WSTG scenario WSTG-ATHZ-05.\n- VulnerabilityClass anchors Insecure Direct Object Reference, Broken Object-Level Authorization map to WSTG scenario WSTG-ATHZ-05.\n- AttackTechnique anchors Object ID Tampering map to WSTG scenario WSTG-ATHZ-05.\n- ObservableSignal anchors Cross-Tenant Object Access map to WSTG scenario WSTG-ATHZ-05.\n\n## Relation Briefs\n\nPurpose: preserve source-grounded operational claims connecting testing methods, target conditions, defensive behavior, weakness categories, and payload examples.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nOAuth2.0 (hereinafter referred to as OAuth) is an authorization framework that allows a client to access resources on the behalf of its user.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nIn order to achieve this, OAuth heavily relies on tokens to communicate between the different entities, each entity having a different role:\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\n- **Resource Owner:** The entity who grants access to a resource, the owner, and in most cases is the user themselves\n- **Client:** The application that is requesting access to a resource on behalf of the Resource Owner. These clients come in two types:\n    - **Public:** clients that can't protect a secret (*e.g.* frontend focused applications, such as SPAs, mobile applications, etc.)\n    - **Confidential:** clients that are able to securely authenticate with the authorization server by keeping their registered secrets safe (*e.g.* backend services)\n- **Authorization Server:** The server that holds authorization information and grants the access\n- **Resource Server:** The application that serves the content accessed by the client\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nSince OAuth's responsibility is to delegate access rights by the owner to the client, this is a very attractive target for attackers, and bad implementations lead to unauthorized access to the users' resources and information.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nIn order to provide access to a client application, OAuth relies on several authorization grant types to generate an access token:"}
{"reference_id": "2", "content": "Anchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\n*: The implicit flow in OAuth only is deprecated, yet is still a viable solution within Open ID Connect (OIDC) to retrieve `id_tokens`. Be careful to understand how the implicit flow is being used, which can be identified if only the `/authorization` route location is being used to gain an access token, without relying on `/token` route location in any way. An example on this can be found here.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\n*Please note that OAuth flows are a complex topic, and the above includes only a summary of the key areas. The inline references contain further information about the specific flows.*\n\n## Test Objectives\n\nPurpose: Explicit objectives a tester should satisfy for this WSTG scenario.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\n- Determine if OAuth2 implementation is vulnerable or using a deprecated or custom implementation.\n\n## Attack Methods\n\nPurpose: Concrete testing procedures, probes, exploitation approaches, and technique variants.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nDeprecated grant types were obsoleted for security and functionality reasons. Identifying if they're being used allows us to quickly review if they're susceptible to any of the threats pertaining to their usage. Some might be out of scope to the attacker, such as the way a client might be using the users' credentials. This should be documented and raised to the internal engineering teams.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nThe following URL request input fields indicate the OAuth flow being used:\n\n## Prerequisites And Environment\n\nPurpose: Target conditions, reachable inputs, side channels, and applicability constraints.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nDepending on the flow, OAuth transports several types of credentials in as URL request input fields.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\n- Step through the authorization process and identify any credentials present in the URL.\n- If any external resources are included in a page involved with the OAuth flow, analyze the request made to them. Credentials could be leaked in the referrer header.\n\n## Defenses And Detections\n\nPurpose: Controls, validation behavior, mitigations, detection signals, and defensive limits.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\n- When implementing OAuth, always consider the technology used and whether the application is a server-side application that can avoid revealing secrets, or a client-side application that cannot.\n- In almost any case, use the Authorization Code flow with PKCE. One exception may be machine-to-machine flows.\n- Use POST request input fields or header values to transport secrets.\n- When no other possibilities exists (for example, in legacy applications that can not be migrated), implement additional security headers such as a `Referrer-Policy`.\n\n## Code And Payload Examples\n\nPurpose: Payloads, URLs, SQL snippets, command examples, and concrete request material.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nFor public clients, it is generally possible to identify the grant type in the request to the `/token` route location. It is indicated in the token exchange with the parameter `grant_type`.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nThe following example shows the Authorization Code grant with PKCE.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\n```http\nPOST /oauth/token HTTP/1.1\nHost: as.example.com\n[...]\n\n{\n  \"client_id\":\"example-client\",\n  \"code_verifier\":\"example\",\n  \"grant_type\":\"authorization_code\",\n  \"code\":\"example\",\n  \"redirect_uri\":\"<scheme>://<host>/<path>\n}\n```\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nThe values for the `grant_type` parameter and the grant type they indicate are:\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\n- `password`: Indicates the ROPC grant.\n- `client_credentials`: Indicates the Client Credential grant.\n- `authorization_code`: Indicates the Authorization Code grant.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nThe Implicit Flow type is not indicated by the `grant_type` parameter since the token is presented in the response to the `/authorization` route location request, and instead can be identified through the `response_type`. Below is an example."}
{"reference_id": "1", "content": "Anchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-1; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\n```http\nGET /callback?code=INJECT_CODE_HERE&state=example HTTP/1.1\nHost: client.example.com\n[...]\n\n```\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-1; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nUnder certain circumstances the PKCE extension can be removed from the authorization code flow. This has the potential to leave public clients vulnerable to attacks mitigated by the PKCE extension.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-1; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\n1. Send the authorization request without the `code_challenge=sha256(xyz)` and `code_challenge_method` parameter.\n2. Send the authorization request with an empty value for the `code_challenge=sha256(xyz)` parameter.\n3. Send the authorization request with a forged value for the `code_challenge=sha256(xyz)` parameter\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-1; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\n```http\nGET /authorize\n    ?redirect_uri=http%3A%2F%client.example.com\n    &client_id=example-client\n    &errorPath=%2Ferror\n    &scope=openid%20profile%20email\n    &response_type=code\n    &response_mode=web_message\n    &state=example-state\n    &nonce=example-nonce\n    &code_challenge=MODIFY_OR_OMIT_THIS\n    &code_challenge_method=MODIFY_OR_OMIT_THIS\n    &prompt=none HTTP/1.1\nHost: as.example.com\n[...]\n\n```\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-1; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nThe AS should verify the `code_verifier` value in the token exchange. To test:\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-1; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\n1. Send the token request without the `code_verifier`.\n2. Send the token request with an empty `code_verifier`.\n3. Send the token request with a valid `code_verifier` for a different authorization code.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-1; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\n```http\nPOST /oauth/token HTTP/1.1\nHost: as.example.com\n[...]\n\n{\n\"client_id\":\"example-client\",\n\"code_verifier\":\"MODIFY_OR_OMIT_THIS\",\n\"code\":\"example\",\n\"grant_type\":\"authorization_code\",\n\"redirect_uri\":\"<scheme>://<host>/<path>\n}\n```\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-1; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nTo prevent CSRF attacks OAuth, leverages the `state` parameter as an anti-CSRF token.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-1; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\n```http\nPOST /u/consent?state=Tampered_State HTTP/1.1\nHost: as.example.com\n[...]\n\nstate=MODIFY_OR_OMIT_THIS\n&audience=https%3A%2F%2Fas.example.com%2Fuserinfo\n&scope%5B%5D=profile\n&scope%5B%5D=email\n&action=accept\n```\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-1; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\nClickjacking is described in Testing for Clickjacking. When the consent page is prone to clickjacking and the attacker is in possession of the `client_id` (for public clients, or the client secret for confidential clients), the attacker can forge the user's consent and gain access to the requested resource through a rogue client.\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-1; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\n```html\n<html>\n    <head>\n        <title>Clickjack test page</title>\n    </head>\n    <body>\n        <iframe src=\"<scheme>://<host>/<path> width=\"500\" height=\"500\"></iframe>\n    </body>\n</html>\n```\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-1; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\n```http\nGET /userinfo HTTP/1.1\nHost: as.example.com\n[...]\nAuthorization: Bearer eyJhbGciOiJkaXIiL[...]\n\n```\n\nAnchor: WSTG ID WSTG-ATHZ-05-1; WSTG title Testing for OAuth Authorization Server Weaknesses; WSTG category Authorization Testing.\n```http\nPOST /token HTTP/1.1\nHost: as.example.com\nCookie: [...]\n[...]"}
```

