{"entity": "Reachable Login Workflow", "type": "PreconditionEnvironment", "description": "Reachable Login Workflow is defined as a target condition wherein an interactive authentication interface or user login process is accessible to an external entity like a security tester or adversary. This state is characterized by the presence of a functional login form or credential submission endpoint that is publicly discoverable, responsive to user input, and capable of receiving authentication attempts. It provides an operational pathway that allows for the submission of credentials or automated probes to evaluate the strength of user authentication methods, session management controls, and enforcement logic. Serving as a prerequisite condition for security testing, this accessible workflow facilitates vulnerability detection—such as testing brute force resistance or identifying session token leakage—and commonly acts as a starting point for further network exploitation or step chaining within a penetration testing framework. Ultimately, it signifies an attack surface where a login endpoint is reachable, operational, and ready for inspection and manipulation, enabling password testing and other authentication-related security assessments.<SEP>An active login sequence that an attacker can interact with to manipulate credentials or session state.<SEP>A target login page or authentication flow that is available and reachable via HTTP/HTTPS on the internal network.<SEP>An existing login workflow that is reachable during testing."}
