{"entity": "Session Cookie", "type": "Artifact", "description": "Session Cookie is a text-based token stored in the browser that serves as a client-side means to pass session-identifying data between the browser and server. It is used by web applications to maintain user session state across stateless HTTP requests, thereby allowing users to remain logged in and authenticated without re-submitting credentials for every interaction. Typically stored as an HTTP cookie, it contains information such as a session identifier, expiration time, and sometimes security flags that determine user role, privileges, or administrative access. The cookie operates by being added to HTTP requests—commonly via headers—as a small piece of data that the server can use to recognize and associate requests with a specific session. While session cookies support continuous access during an active session, they are designed to be deleted automatically upon the browser closing or the user-initiated session ending.<SEP>Persisted token stored by browser used for session identification.<SEP>Information stored by the browser and automatically included in requests to the originating domain to maintain session state.<SEP>A browser-stored token carrying session information used by the application for authentication.<SEP>Identifiable token used by the web application to maintain authenticated user state.<SEP>An identifier transmitted by the server via HTTP Set-Cookie headers to maintain user session state.<SEP>A token stored by the browser that maintains user session state across multiple requests for the same origin or domain group."}
