{"entity": "Executable Upload Webshell", "type": "AttackTechnique", "description": "Executable Upload Webshell refers to a cyberattack technique that exploits insufficient input validation in file upload mechanisms of web servers. Attackers upload a malicious payload, which typically takes the form of scripts or binaries, to a writable location that remains accessible via web requests. This successful deployment allows the adversary to achieve unauthorized remote code execution, effectively granting them the capability to issue system commands as authenticated users. Such a compromise establishes persistent remote access to the server, enabling further malicious activities including privilege escalation, lateral movement, and data exfiltration. Commonly, this attack involves uploading malicious scripts—sometimes disguised as benign files such as images—to locations facilitating their execution, often utilizing path traversal vulnerabilities to ensure persistence. By planting web shells that remain accessible via web requests, adversaries maintain ongoing control over the compromised host.<SEP>An action involving uploading and initiating a web shell to grant attacker code execution on the server.<SEP>Technique that uploads malicious web scripts and executes them through a web server component with upload or writable path access.<SEP>A method of submitting and saving malicious scripts or binary executables into a web application so they can be remotely invoked later.<SEP>Reusable malicious input structured to bypass upload restrictions and allow placing arbitrary code for remote execution.<SEP>An action uploading malicious scripts or binaries to a target system and executing them via an accessible handler.<SEP>An attack technique that uploads executable files through web interfaces to gain persistent, privileged access to a host."}
