{"entity": "Server-Side URL Fetch Feature", "type": "PreconditionEnvironment", "description": "Server-Side URL Fetch Feature is a server capability that allows backend code to send outbound HTTP or HTTPS requests to external or internal endpoints based on user input, application logic, or other dynamic components. It enables the dynamic loading of resources and facilitates flexible integration with other systems by allowing URLs to be specified dynamically rather than hard-coded. However, this feature presents significant security risks, including the potential for Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF) attacks if input is not properly sanitized or validated. Consequently, server-side URL fetch implementations must incorporate robust input validation and strict access controls to mitigate these threats while preserving the utility of the feature for legitimate backend services and dynamic content retrieval. The capability is realized through server components configured to perform URL fetching or redirects when dynamic parameters are provided, and encompasses functions that use external URL API calls to process input before returning a response or rendering dynamic content.<SEP>A target capability to make outbound HTTP(S) requests from the server side.<SEP>A server mechanism where user-controlled input is passed into an HTTP or URL-fetching function.<SEP>A server-side capability to dynamically fetch external or internal URLs based on user-supplied paths or parameters.<SEP>Server capability to perform outbound HTTP(S) requests triggered by user input or application logic.<SEP>A target functionality that performs server-side HTTP requests."}
{"entity": "Reachable Login Workflow", "type": "PreconditionEnvironment", "description": "Reachable Login Workflow is defined as a target condition wherein an interactive authentication interface or user login process is accessible to an external entity like a security tester or adversary. This state is characterized by the presence of a functional login form or credential submission endpoint that is publicly discoverable, responsive to user input, and capable of receiving authentication attempts. It provides an operational pathway that allows for the submission of credentials or automated probes to evaluate the strength of user authentication methods, session management controls, and enforcement logic. Serving as a prerequisite condition for security testing, this accessible workflow facilitates vulnerability detection—such as testing brute force resistance or identifying session token leakage—and commonly acts as a starting point for further network exploitation or step chaining within a penetration testing framework. Ultimately, it signifies an attack surface where a login endpoint is reachable, operational, and ready for inspection and manipulation, enabling password testing and other authentication-related security assessments.<SEP>An active login sequence that an attacker can interact with to manipulate credentials or session state.<SEP>A target login page or authentication flow that is available and reachable via HTTP/HTTPS on the internal network.<SEP>An existing login workflow that is reachable during testing."}
{"entity": "Localhost-Only Service Exists", "type": "PreconditionEnvironment", "description": "The technique is applicable only when an internal service bound to localhost is exposed via an SSRF vector.<SEP>Localhost-Only Service Exists refers to an internal service strictly bound to loopback network interfaces such as `127.0.0.1` or `::1`, intended to isolate system functionality from direct external network access. Because these services are restricted to being reachable only from processes running on the same host, they are commonly deployed to support internal system operations without broad internet exposure. Despite this intended logical isolation, such configurations frequently present attack surfaces that can be exploited via Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF). Attackers can bypass the localhost restriction by abusing other network interfaces, APIs, or misconfigurations to compromise the service from outside the host. Consequently, a localhost-bound service remains a potential vulnerability whenever it can be accessed by an attacker through indirect means, even if external networks cannot directly reach it. The security of these services therefore depends heavily on stringent controls that prevent unintended or malicious external interaction, as the localhost binding alone does not offer sufficient protection against sophisticated bypass techniques."}
{"entity": "Path Traversal", "type": "VulnerabilityClass", "description": "Path Traversal is a cybersecurity weakness wherein an attacker exploits specially-crafted input containing directory traversal sequences—most commonly the `../` pattern—to access files, directories, or resources located outside the intended directory structure or web root. This vulnerability occurs when user-supplied path components are not adequately sanitized, thereby allowing the attacker to bypass access controls and navigate the file system beyond authorized boundaries. By injecting relative path traversal sequences such as `../../` into input used to construct file paths, an attacker can attempt unauthorized reads, writes, or execution of sensitive or critical resources outside their intended scope. At its core, this issue stems from insufficient validation of user input during file path construction, which may lead to the exposure of confidential data or full system compromise. This vulnerability often permits access to any arbitrary directories or files on the target filesystem, enabling breaches through manipulation of path references with special characters or traversal sequences that exceed the permitted web root.<SEP>An input handling weakness where directory navigation characters bypass intended path restrictions.<SEP>Attack surface allowing directory traversal references (e.g., `../../../`) to access files or resources outside intended directories.<SEP>A flaw where user-controlled input is improperly used in file system paths to access unauthorized files or directories.<SEP>A weakness class enabling an attacker to access files or directories outside the intended web root by manipulating URL or input paths.<SEP>A weakness in file system handling that allows access to files outside the intended directory."}
