{"entity": "WinRM Credential Login", "type": "AttackTechnique", "description": "WinRM Credential Login refers to a technique used to authenticate to Windows Remote Management (WinRM) services by transmitting credentials during the handshake process. This methodology is employed to gain initial access to target endpoints, often followed by further command execution or privilege escalation activities. By leveraging either valid, harvested, or guessed credentials, adversaries can establish sessions and interact with the system remotely. This approach is utilized in both penetration testing and malicious exploitation scenarios to bypass authentication mechanisms and obtain command shell access on Windows-based systems.<SEP>An authentication technique that injects valid credentials during WinRM communications to test privilege escalation or lateral movement.<SEP>A technique using Windows Remote Management to authenticate over HTTP(S) with misconfigured credential checks."}
{"entity": "Verbose Error Disclosure", "type": "PreconditionEnvironment", "description": "Verbose Error Disclosure describes a software behavior where applications or servers return overly detailed error responses to the client. This information typically includes sensitive backend data such as file paths, function names, variable names, stack traces, server types, database schema details, or specific SQL syntax. The disclosure frequently occurs when the system processes invalid or malformed input but fails to apply security filtering, allowing internal implementation specifics—intended only for developers—to become visible to unauthorized users. By revealing private data including system configuration and application logic, verbose error disclosure can expose the underlying system architecture, creating vulnerabilities that may be exploited in further attacks or reverse engineering efforts. This phenomenon functions as an observable signal indicating potential misconfigurations in error handling that compromise application security and internal data privacy."}
{"entity": "JWT Token Forgery", "type": "AttackTechnique", "description": "JWT Token Forgery is the act of creating, modifying, or exploiting JSON Web Tokens to impersonate legitimate users or circumvent authorization checks within a system. It involves generating malicious tokens containing false claims or forged digital signatures that successfully validate against the issuer or relying party, thereby enabling unauthorized access or privilege escalation. While this technique can be employed intentionally for security testing purposes to evaluate the effectiveness of authentication and authorization controls, when executed maliciously it represents a critical vulnerability in systems depending on JWTs for access management. Attackers who exploit this method generate or tamper with counterfeit JSON Web Tokens to bypass bearer token authentication controls or inject falsified data into valid tokens, allowing them to operate as authenticated users without possessing legitimate credentials. This is achieved by exploiting implementation flaws, weaknesses in the handling of secret keys, or insufficient token validation flows.<SEP>Technique that constructs or modifies JSON web tokens to impersonate a user or bypass authorization checks due to secret weakness or flow flaws.<SEP>A method involving creating or modifying valid-looking JSON Web Tokens to impersonate users, elevate roles, or bypass session checks.<SEP>Technique to craft or alter JSON Web Tokens to impersonate an authenticated user.<SEP>A method for creating fraudulent JSON Web Tokens to impersonate a legitimate user within the application."}
{"entity": "Source Code Review", "type": "AttackTechnique", "description": "Source Code Review is a systematic technique for analyzing source code—typically from public repositories, exposed APIs, or extracted builds—to identify security vulnerabilities, sensitive information disclosures, and configuration weaknesses. It involves methodical scrutiny of code files to detect hard-coded credentials, misconfigurations, improper trust boundaries, injection points, and insecure implementation flaws. This process allows organizations to uncover logic weaknesses, hidden capabilities, and exploitable components before they can be leveraged by attackers. By extracting configuration data, secrets, and identifying potential attack vectors such as privilege escalation or sensitive data leaks, source code review informs risk mitigation strategies and strengthens the overall security posture of software systems. It is employed both defensively by security teams to remediate issues proactively and offensively by threat actors to map potential exploitation paths, making it a critical component in application security testing and software development lifecycle security practices.<SEP>A testing activity analyzing accessible source code repositories to discover hardcoded credentials, logic flaws, or path traversal opportunities."}
