{"entity": "Authenticated Session", "type": "PreconditionEnvironment", "description": "Requires valid session tokens but access often grants authenticated context where further actions require session integrity.<SEP>Authenticated Session is an active user login session on a target system, established with valid credentials that have been verified as legitimate. Such a session maintains continuity and provides the necessary context to request and access restricted resources, systems, or functionalities that would otherwise be unavailable to un-authenticated users. In cybersecurity contexts, this authenticated state is often sought by attackers to conduct post-exploitation maneuvers, extend their foothold, or escalate privileges by abusing the legitimate identity or access permissions associated with the session. Typically manifested as session tokens, stored state, or an active user context, an authenticated session grants permission to interact with system resources under the assumed identity of the verified user, making it a critical element in both normal system operation and malicious activities such as lateral movement and privilege abuse.<SEP>Active session with valid authentications that already authenticated and can be leveraged for further requests.<SEP>An established session with valid user credentials providing access to protected features.<SEP>The attacker already possesses a valid session cookie or token proving user credentials<SEP>Presence of a valid user session token that can impersonate the user for API or web requests.<SEP>An active session with valid user or service authentication credentials that maintains session state across requests for continued exploitation.<SEP>An active session token or HTTP cookie that maintains state for an already-authenticated user."}
{"entity": "Privilege Escalation Path", "type": "AttackerCapability", "description": "Provides steps to reach higher permissions by pivoting through systems authenticated by cracked credentials.<SEP>Privilege Escalation Path refers to a reusable sequence of steps, techniques, or conditions that allow an attacker to progress from lower system privileges to higher privileges or full control of a target system. It represents a feasible chain of exploitable weaknesses, misconfigurations, or trusted relationships that together facilitate unauthorized access elevation. Often serving as a pivot point following initial compromise—for example via web shells in Windows Active Directory environments—this pathway underpins broader attacks aimed at achieving domain-wide control or root access. The term describes a strategic objective to increase an attacker's effective permissions beyond their initial constrained role, moving toward administrative or root-level access within the target environment. A Privilege Escalation Path constitutes a sequence of actions, discovered conditions, or interconnected vulnerabilities that permit the systematic accumulation of higher-level system permissions after initial access is obtained. This sequence enables lateral movement, expanded operational ability, and ultimately grants the user permissions more extensive than originally permitted, thereby facilitating unauthorized control. Acquired credentials such as valid user hashes further support this progression by allowing the attacker to authenticate and perform subsequent privilege escalation steps when encountering accounts with higher scopes or admin roles.<SEP>A pre-existing method or sequence of steps the attacker can follow to elevate their permissions from an initial low-privilege context to SYSTEM or root level.<SEP>A sequence of vulnerabilities or permission misconfigurations that can lead to gaining higher administrative privileges.<SEP>A sequence of actions or existing session state